  "strategy": "Through careful consideration, a different optimization approach using Covariance Matrix Adaptation Evolution Strategy (CMA-ES) is proposed. This method is well-suited for non-linear optimization problems and allows working with unconstrained normalized sequences and projecting them into the constrained space during evaluation. The key improvements are:\n\n1. Approach and rationale\nAll previous methods could be beat with a better optimization strategy and suitable sequence families. This approach combines several features:\n- Explicit sequence normalization by sum rather than length\n- Clamping during evaluation to [0,1000]\n- Purpose-specific sequences including uniform, cosine, autocorrelated and segmented profiles\n- Global optimization with CMA-ES for non-linear, inseparable objective function\n- Multi-resolution explorations matching the characteristic ceiling of 1.5\n\nThe core idea is to minimize the normalized squared convolution kernel sum, achieved by cosine-modulated sequences that pack the signal representation optimally.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe search minimizes \u0393(a) = n * ||a||\u209a\u00b2 / ||f*f||\u221e\u2097\u00b2 with the following constraints:\n- a \u2265 0\n- ||a|| \u2264 1\n- ||a*a||\u221e is restricted to approximate constant\n\nRepresented mathematically:\n\u0393(a) \u2254 2.0 * n * max(convolve(a)) / (sum(a))\u00b2\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nStep-by-step algorithm:\n\nA. Input initialization\nInitialize:\n  - Run-time monitoring timer\n  - n from arguments\n  - Candidate sequence generation (5 stages)\n  - Best candidate storage\n\nB. Sequence generation\nDefine specialized sequences:\n  - ```text.insert(UniformInitialization, None)```\n  - ```text.insert(CosineInitialization, None)```\n  - ```text.insert(LinearSegmentation, None)```\n  - ```text.insert(SparseChirplet, None)```\n  - ```text.insert(RandomPhaseSines, None)```\n\nC. Optimization phase (CMA-ES)\nLoop:\n  Check remaining time\n  For each novel sequence pattern:\n    Generate samples from distribution\n    Evaluate \u0393(a)\n    Update best candidate\n    Project results into constrained space\n\nD. Candidate acceptancet\nCompare using:\n  - Fitness score from evaluate_sequence()\n  - Random sampling around best points (30 times)\n  - Handle boundary conditions via projection\n\nE. Best candidate selection\nAfter time expiration:\n  Project best candidate from normalized space\n  Ensure coefficients clamped to [0,1000]\n  Return 2*n elements\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nBootstrap configurations include:\n\nFamily   |       | Statistical properties | Approximated min score |\n---:      +---       |                      | ---:                    |\nCosine       | | High    | Low improvement         |\n            | variability          |       possibility          |\nUniform      | | Medium  | High                      |\n            | diversity           |\nSegmented    | | High    | Medium                    |\nLinear       | |          |\n\nBest configuration identified via performance sweeps\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nEstimate:\n  - n=2048 required ~10 evaluations initially\n  - Remaining time checks before each major step\n  - Automatically terminate edges cases related to max 1.5 ceiling\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nEnsure:\n  - Coefficient positivity via projection\n  - Non-infinity score via final verification\n  - Output format matches Python list requirement\n  - Response under 2400 seconds via careful scheduling\n\n\nFinal candidate preparation:\n  project_final = project_simplex(best_candidate)\n  project_final = [min(1000, x) for x in project_final]\n  score_final = float(evaluate_sequence(project_final))\n\nReturn formatted candidate list",
